in this episode of cloud performance  atlas I show how to extend tracing to  every aspect of your cloud native  application we'll our performance stay  inside the lines stay tuned to find out  one of my favorite things about managed  services like App Engine is that your  requests are automatically traced  letting you look at a given request and  see where various execution time is  going without you having to write any  code yourself sadly if you wanted the  same sort of data for your non managed  services it meant a lot of custom code  custom deployments and a whole new team  to write a custom dashboard UI for you  but not anymore stackdriver has recently  released a library which allows your  non-managed code to get the same tracing  awesomeness with less hassle to you  using the newly created open census  library you can get instrumentation data  from any VMs or containers running in  your application across a number of  languages including Java Python nodejs  golang and more heck you could even run  it on your non Google cloud services and  get that data to the client-side library  works by patching well-known modules in  your application in order to label and  measure latency of our pcs and incoming  requests and for the not so well-known  functions you can also add custom spans  to your code they catch the important  bits that fall between all that data is  then collected and displayed in a nice  consistent UI in the cloud console to  help you track down where your  performance is going if you're  interested in getting started with stack  driver tracing check out the article  linked in the description below and  don't forget to subscribe to our YouTube  channel for more great cloud performance  Atlas content because when it comes to  performance every millisecond counts
 

